# Build and check hygiene

## Warm builds (high-priority standing policy)

Assume every build will fail at least once and will be run again. Plan each
build (app, service, image, package, firmware, container) so repeating it costs
minutes, not a full cold rebuild.

- **State where warm state lives before building:** Before starting or
  proposing any multi-minute build, state what persists between runs (unpacked
  sources, build dir, compiler/download caches), where it lives, and the
  expected repeat cost. Build cold only when unavoidable and agreed.
- **Preserve build outputs across downstream failures:** Separate build from
  packaging, signing, publishing, and install so downstream failures retry from
  verified build output rather than rebuilding.
- **Fail fast and report everything:** Run cheap checks before expensive ones
  and replay rule/script changes against fixtures before paying for a full
  build.
- **Measure builds:** Record phase durations, build steps executed, and cache
  hits, and report whether each build was warm or cold.

## Run every check to the end

Test builds and CI runs are expensive, so one run must surface every problem
together instead of stopping at the first failure. Use keep-going flags
(`cargo test --no-fail-fast`, `fail-fast: false` in CI matrices, and check
scripts that collect all failures before exiting non-zero). Stop early only
when a later step is meaningless or unsafe, and report what was skipped.
